{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ALSO MAP THE LENGTH LIMITS — five hardcoded constants, none empirically confirmed on DC\n\nCheap to check and it closes a class the vocabulary checks miss. The constants:\n\n    JIRA_SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS   254     jira_family/value_maps.py:57\n    JIRA_LABEL_MAX_CHARS     255     jira_family/value_maps.py:58\n    WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT   32767   jira_family/rich_text.py:60      (DC description)\n    _JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS  32767   adapters/jira/comment_limits.py:41\n    _ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT   32000   adapters/jira/adf.py:332         (Cloud, margin under 32767)\n\nEach is a documented-or-inferred number, not a measured one. A limit that is too HIGH means we send\na body Jira rejects — and bug 6afc records that **ACLI exits 0 on an over-length rejection**, so it\nfails silently and re-emits every pass. A limit that is too LOW means we truncate content the\ninstance would have accepted, quietly losing user text. Both are silent; neither has a live check.\n\n### The DC comment ceiling is CONFIGURABLE, which makes a hardcoded constant especially fragile\n\nBug 049e established that DC's comment limit comes from the instance property\n`jira.text.field.character.limit`, with `0` meaning UNLIMITED. So the right value is not a constant\nat all — it is instance state that the map job should READ, and that the declared environment\ncontract should then pin. If the harness image ships a different value than 32767, our fitting logic\nis calibrated to the wrong number and no current test would say so.\n\n### How to measure, per field\n\nFor `summary`, `description`, `comment` and `label`, write at **limit-1, limit, and limit+1** and\nrecord the instance's actual response for each:\n\n * accepted intact → the limit is at least this\n * rejected with an error → the real ceiling, and the error shape (which matters: a silent 0-exit\n   rejection is a different defect from a 4xx)\n * **accepted but SILENTLY TRUNCATED** → the most dangerous outcome and the one no unit test can\n   see; detect it by reading the value back and comparing lengths, never by trusting the write\n\nThat read-back is the load-bearing part. Accept-and-truncate looks identical to success at the write.\n\n### One subtlety worth carrying over\n\nCloud measures the description limit against the **ADF-serialized** size, not the plain text —\n`_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` is 32000 rather than 32767 precisely to leave margin, and a 32,767-char\ndescription was observed serializing to ~50,566 ADF chars. DC's `WikiTextCodec` is a plain character\ntruncation instead. So the map should record **which representation the instance counts** for DC,\nnot assume it matches either. That question is live right now: a peer session is designing\nMarkdown⇄wiki conversion ([rebar:a32a-4549-3eb9-4fb9]), and length-fit measured on the wrong\nrepresentation is exactly the kind of mismatch that survives a green unit suite.\n\n### Acceptance addition\n\n- [ ] each of the five limits above is measured live at limit-1 / limit / limit+1, with the raw\n      response recorded, and any divergence from the hardcoded constant reported as a finding\n- [ ] `jira.text.field.character.limit` is read from the instance and recorded in the contract\n      rather than assumed\n- [ ] the accept-and-truncate case is detected by READ-BACK comparison, not inferred from a\n      successful write"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785798501202615001,"uuid":"4c3d9747-ef84-4221-bd3f-dfd1c99772a6"}